  "role": "execute",
  "tier": "sol",
  "complexity": 4,
  "title": "B1: one sqlite connection per thread in bus.db(); read() skips rows whose task file is missing",
  "spec": "Goal T-1388. orchestrator/bus.py db() (bus.py:58) caches a single sqlite3 connection in module globals (_connection, _connection_path) with check_same_thread=False and shares it across the daemon and its worker threads; read() (bus.py:~195) runs `select id from tasks order by id` outside locked() and then get(r[0]) for each row. Worker threads intermittently die with KeyError('') or KeyError(None) from get(r[0]) although the table never holds such rows, consistent with interleaved statements on the shared connection.\nChange:\n1. db() returns a connection per thread: keep them in a threading.local keyed by path (same create-table setup, isolation_level=None). Keep _close_db working for tests and atexit (close the calling thread's connection; connections of exited threads may be closed lazily or left to GC). No behaviour change for single-threaded callers.\n2. read(): skip any row whose id is not a non-empty str, and skip (do not raise) rows whose task file is missing (KeyError from get), so a row mid-write never kills a caller; count skipped rows and, if any, append one stderr line \"bus.read: skipped N unreadable rows\".\nTests in tests/test_bus.py (unittest.TestCase methods): concurrent reads and writes from 8 threads for ~200 iterations each never raise and always return well-formed task dicts; db() returns distinct connection objects in two threads and the same object within one thread; read() skips a row inserted into the tasks table without a task file and still returns the others. No new dependencies.",
  "inputs": [],
  "acceptance": [
    "tests/test_bus.py::test_concurrent_reads_and_writes_never_raise",
    "tests/test_bus.py::test_db_connection_is_per_thread",
    "tests/test_bus.py::test_read_skips_rows_without_task_file",
    ".claude/hooks/tests-green.sh wt/<this task> exits 0"
  ],
  "scope": [
